WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.700
What happens when a program

2
00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:03.660
makes a system call? A

3
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.100
system call is a controlled

4
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.780
request from a user program

5
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.500
to a protected operating-system service.

6
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.780
It connects application needs to

7
00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.720
privileged work without giving the

8
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.780
application unrestricted control of the

9
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:16.080
hardware.

10
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.560
Start with the course example:

11
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.920
an application calls the C

12
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.460
library function F open. That

13
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.660
library function can perform user-space

14
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.340
work and eventually invoke the

15
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.680
open system call. The library

16
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.580
interface and the kernel entry

17
00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.820
are related layers, not two

18
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.040
names for one identical instruction.

19
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.020
The application supplies arguments such

20
00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.620
as a pathname and access

21
00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.940
mode. A user-space stub follows

22
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:44.660
the operating system calling convention

23
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.380
to identify the requested service

24
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.460
and arrange its arguments. The

25
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.360
exact registers and instruction depend

26
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.020
on the architecture and operating

27
00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:52.500
system.

28
00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.100
In the supplied xv6 lab,

29
00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:56.900
the stub uses a system-call

30
00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.420
number and an ecall instruction

31
00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.320
to enter the kernel. That

32
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:01.600
creates a trap rather than

33
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.460
an ordinary function call across

34
00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.720
the protection boundary. Kernel trap

35
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.180
handling then reaches the numbered

36
00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.500
system-call dispatcher.

37
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.100
The dispatcher selects the matching

38
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.700
kernel handler. That handler checks

39
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.120
the request and performs the

40
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.940
permitted service, potentially using file-system

41
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.940
or device layers. User-provided values

42
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.940
cannot simply be trusted because

43
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:24.360
they arrived through a legitimate

44
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.260
entry instruction.

45
00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.420
After the service, a result

46
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.600
is placed according to the

47
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.700
calling convention and execution returns

48
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.500
to user mode. The result

49
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.160
might be a file descriptor

50
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.120
or an error indication. The

51
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.880
application continues after the call,

52
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.140
unless the requested operation blocks

53
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.680
or changes that control flow.

54
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.920
A blocking request can make

55
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.460
the caller wait while another

56
00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.680
process runs. Later the event

57
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.340
makes the caller ready, and

58
00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.740
the scheduler may select it

59
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.420
again. That is why a

60
00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.180
system call can involve scheduling,

61
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.240
while a fast successful call

62
00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.860
may return without switching to

63
00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:58.600
another process.

64
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.920
Compare a device interrupt with

65
00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.520
the system call. A device

66
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.780
interrupt reports an asynchronous hardware

67
00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.480
event; a system call is

68
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.220
a deliberate service request by

69
00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.920
the running program. Both may

70
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.860
enter kernel handling, but their

71
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:13.500
origins and purposes differ.

72
00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.720
Not every library function invokes

73
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.480
a system call every time

74
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.700
it runs. A calculation may

75
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.740
remain entirely in user space,

76
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.180
and buffered input-output can defer

77
00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.400
kernel requests. In the course

78
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.380
model, a system call uses

79
00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.220
a controlled trap, but an

80
00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.620
ordinary API invocation is not

81
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.020
proof that a trap occurred.

82
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.700
Things to take note of.

83
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.720
Trace application, library, stub, trap,

84
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.120
dispatcher, handler and return in

85
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.660
order. Explain which stage changes

86
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.020
privilege, distinguish asynchronous interrupts, and

87
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.300
never claim that a file

88
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.100
descriptor is the file contents

89
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:52.620
themselves.
